---
import BrandLayout from "../layouts/BrandLayout.astro";
---

<BrandLayout active="about" title="About | Zachary Lewis" description="Zachary Lewis went from building a 100K+ audience in video to shipping agent systems that run real operations.">
  <!-- Header -->
  <section class="border-b border-ink">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-20 md:py-28 grid md:grid-cols-5 gap-10 items-center">
      <div class="md:col-span-3">
        <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">About</span>
        <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold text-ink leading-[1.05]">
          From 25M views to systems that run the business.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed max-w-xl">
          I'm Zachary Lewis — a builder based in Detroit. I build software that runs without me.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-2">
        <div class="aspect-square w-full max-w-sm rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-ink bg-paper2">
          <img src="/profile-photo.jpg" alt="Zachary Lewis" class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Story — left-aligned to the same axis as the headline above -->
  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-[68ch] text-ink/80 text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-5">
      <p>
        For years, storytelling was the front door. I built a video production business in Detroit and grew an
        audience of <strong>100K+ followers and 25M+ views</strong>, while quietly building the websites, tools,
        and automations my clients actually ran on. Video taught me the thing most engineers never have to learn:
        nobody owes you their attention. If the first ten seconds don't earn it, the rest doesn't exist. That turns
        out to be true of software too.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2024 I turned all of it toward agent systems. Since then I've shipped an agent platform that handles the day-to-day
        across my own companies, a trading workspace where members bring their own broker, a laundry marketplace
        running in Detroit, a self-enriching map of the city's small businesses, and a support platform for
        families of people who are incarcerated.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last one taught me the most. I'd built a health platform that worked and that nobody used. Rather than
        start over, I sat in weekly support group meetings with a Detroit reentry organization and listened. Two
        things came up again and again — people needed help writing letters, and they needed help writing resumes.
        So I built both.
      </p>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="my-12 md:my-16 border-t border-b border-ink py-8 md:py-10 max-w-[68ch]">
      <p class="text-2xl md:text-[2rem] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink leading-[1.15]">
        The useful thing is almost never the thing you guessed from your desk.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <div class="max-w-[68ch] text-ink/80 text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-5">
      <p>
        Here's what all of it adds up to. Most people <em>talk</em> about AI; very few make it do the work. The gap
        isn't the models — it's the unglamorous engineering around them. Knowing what a system costs before it runs.
        Keeping one customer's data away from another's. Being able to prove, afterward, whether the thing actually
        worked. I build that part.
      </p>
      <p>
        So if you're a founder or a small team buried in repetitive work — the inbox, the follow-ups, the busywork
        that quietly eats your week — that's what I take off your plate. Not a deck, not a demo that falls apart
        when you click the wrong thing. Software you can open in a browser and watch do the job.
      </p>
      <p>
        If that sounds like what you need, <a href="/contact" class="text-accent font-semibold">let's build something</a>.
        If you'd rather just see how I think, <a href="/selected-work" class="text-accent font-semibold">the long-form
        write-ups</a> go deep on what each system does and what was hard about it — and
        <a href="/tools" class="text-accent font-semibold">the free tools are yours</a>, no signup.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="border-t border-ink bg-paper2">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Let's build something that runs itself.</h2>
      <a href="/contact" class="inline-block px-7 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-ink text-white border border-ink font-medium hover:bg-accent hover:border-accent transition-opacity">
        Get in touch
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BrandLayout>
